-- title:
--   Proposition 4.10 — a hyperfinite graphical game with finitely many relevant neighbours admits a strong Harsányi $\varepsilon$-equilibrium (generalized to a finite-section $D\subseteq G$)
-- statement:
--   Let $(X,G,u)$ be a hyperfinite graphical game on a standard Borel space, and let $D\subseteq G$ be a Borel relation in which every vertex has finitely many $D$-neighbours, such that each payoff $u(g,\cdot)$ depends only on the actions of $g$ and of its $D$-neighbours. Then for every finite measure $\mu$ on $X$ and every $\varepsilon>0$ the game admits a strong Harsányi $\varepsilon$-equilibrium: a Borel profile $\sigma$ and a set $\Omega'$ with $\mu(\Omega')\le\varepsilon$ such that every vertex outside $\Omega'$ optimizes exactly.
--
--   With $D=G$, for a hyperfinite graphical game of finite degree, this is the printed proposition.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: a hyperfinite graphical game of finite degree → a hyperfinite graphical game of countable degree whose payoffs depend only on a Borel D ⊆ G with finite sections; reason: D = G recovers the printed statement; this is the form the corrected Lemma 4.8 delivers
--   - generalization: µ a measure on Ω, used as a probability measure (µ(Ω_N) ≥ 1 − δ) → any finite measure µ; reason: a probability measure is a finite measure
--   - generalization: the vertex set of a graphical game is a Polish space → the vertex set X is a standard Borel space; reason: every Polish space is standard Borel and no notion used depends on the topology of X; the associated game of §4.2 lives on the type space, which is standard Borel without a named Polish topology
--   - encoding: payoff u : Ω × A^Ω → ℝ continuous in the Tychonoff topology on A × A^N(g), extended multilinearly to mixed profiles → u : X → (X → A) → ℝ with each u g continuous on A^X (A discrete) and depending only on g and N(g); the mixed payoff is the integral against the product measure ⊗_h σ(h); reason: given locality, continuity on A^X is continuity on A × A^N(g); the expectation under the product measure is the multilinear extension
--   - encoding: mixed actions in ∆(A) → probability mass functions PMF A; Borel strategies are those with every coordinate g ↦ σ(g)(b) measurable; reason: A is finite, so ∆(A) is the set of probability mass functions on A
--   - encoding: hyperfinite: generated by a Borel ℤ-action, or alternatively an increasing union of Borel finite equivalence relations (§2.3) → the increasing-union form; reason: the paper states the two forms as equivalent for CBERs (Dougherty–Jackson–Kechris 1994) and its proofs use the increasing-union form
-- source:
--   Hellman–Levy, Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence Relation Hierarchy, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Proposition 4.10, p.24 (proof pp.24–25)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Graphical_IsGraphicalGame
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Graphical_IsHyperfiniteGraph
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Graphical_IsStrongHarsanyiEqG

namespace HellmanLevy.Graphical

/-- **Proposition 4.10 (generalized to payoffs depending on a finite-section `D ⊆ G`).** Let
`(X, G, u)` be a hyperfinite graphical game, and suppose that every payoff `u g` depends only on
`g`'s action and the actions of its `D`-neighbours, where `D ⊆ G` is a Borel relation in which
every vertex has finitely many `D`-neighbours. Then for every finite measure `µ` on `X` and
every `ε > 0` the game has a strong Harsányi ε-equilibrium.
Hellman–Levy, *Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence
Relation Hierarchy*, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Proposition 4.10, p.24 ("Every hyperfinite graphical game of finite degree admits a
strong Harsányi ε-equilibrium"; the proof begins "Let µ be a measure on Ω, and let ε > 0").
With `D = G` (`G` of finite degree) this is the printed statement.

**Formalization Note** The generalization is what Lemma 4.8 (corrected) delivers: a game on
the same hyperfinite graph `G` whose payoffs depend on a finite-section `D ⊆ G`. The paper's
measure is a probability measure (its proof uses `µ(Ω_N) ≥ 1 − δ`); any finite measure is
allowed here. -/
theorem proposition_4_10 {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] [StandardBorelSpace X] {A : Type*}
    [Fintype A] [Nonempty A] [MeasurableSpace A] [DiscreteMeasurableSpace A]
    [TopologicalSpace A] [DiscreteTopology A] (G D : X → X → Prop) (u : X → (X → A) → ℝ)
    (μ : MeasureTheory.Measure X) [MeasureTheory.IsFiniteMeasure μ]
    (hG : IsHyperfiniteGraph G) (hu : IsGraphicalGame G u) (hD : CBER.IsBorelRel D)
    (hDG : ∀ x y, D x y → G x y) (hDfin : ∀ x, {y | D x y}.Finite) (huD : DependsOn u D)
    (ε : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) : ∃ σ : X → PMF A, IsStrongHarsanyiEqG u μ ε σ := by sorry

end HellmanLevy.Graphical
